WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.799
Film Courage:&nbsp; Actually, the line I was going
to use was not that line, it was, excuse my

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:08.800
language here, fix your sh$t before you waste
someone else’s time.&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.100
Which I loved (from the movie), that line.&nbsp;

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:16.730
But it can be applied to many things, not
just a relationship and so that is where I

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:19.850
was going with this….Tyler Johnson:&nbsp; That’s
funny you say that, because it’s sort of

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.530
my excuse on why I don’t date right now.&nbsp;

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:28.160
It’s not other people, it’s me fixing….since
we’re now speaking in French…me fixing

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.119
my own sh$t before I just on load on to someone
else.&nbsp;

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:39.300
It does apply to relationships, you want to
be able to give someone your all, you want

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.340
to give them your time and attention, when
you’re after a pursuit like this, your time

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.729
and attention is hard to come by.

00:00:45.729 --> 00:00:46.920
Film Courage:&nbsp; Very true.&nbsp;

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.522
And that’s why I was saying, it is a lonely
journey when you focus solely on something,

00:00:49.522 --> 00:00:52.659
whether it’s an entrepreneur or we’ve
talked to people who were working in tech

00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:55.769
or different similar professions and it is
a lonely thing because people end up falling

00:00:55.769 --> 00:01:00.030
away because you’re busy trying to do stuff
and it’s not because maybe they’re mad

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.609
at you but you just isolate yourself.

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:05.049
Pascal Payant:&nbsp; The best example are the
people that are always surrounded by people,

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:16.890
they are so afraid to face themselves and
that is what I think is the most gratifying.&nbsp;

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:23.070
That when you have that…you can totally
get everything that is on your way and just

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:27.830
break through them but as soon as you are
afraid to face yourself, face your demons,

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.420
there is no way you’re going to be able
to make it in this business.&nbsp;

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.590
You are just going to be pushed left and right
and be so scared and scream “Oh, I want

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:35.590
to quit.&nbsp;

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:39.170
Where’s my Plan B.” Film Courage:&nbsp; And
it can be applied to filmmaking, as well.&nbsp;

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:43.770
So fixing your…whatever…stuff before you….

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.130
Pascal:&nbsp; Being focused.&nbsp;

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.310
That’s the main thing.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:54.009
Tyler:&nbsp; And it’s also, acting is sort of
beautiful in the sense that any problems that

00:01:54.009 --> 00:02:00.250
you have in your personal life will manifest
in your work.&nbsp;

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:06.189
And the weaknesses, the proclivities, all
those things, they show up in the work.&nbsp;

00:02:06.189 --> 00:02:12.500
And so the work becomes sort of the ground
with which you address these things within

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:18.170
yourself and so the take away is pretty remarkable
if you’re really putting yourself into the

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:23.390
work that you’re doing the amount that you
can learn about the way that you approach

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:29.170
problems or the way that you react in situations….I
mean I know I was surprised over the course

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.920
of the film, especially considering going
back to the audition, vulnerability, I mean

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:37.800
how easy it was for me at the end to just
fly off the handle at the character Alyssa.&nbsp;

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:46.200
I mean it was cathartic in a way, but it also
surprised me and it was something since then

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.940
I’ve really gotten to know intimately that
side of myself, a side that maybe I denied

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:55.531
before I met Pascal and did this film.

00:02:55.531 --> 00:03:01.620
Film Courage:&nbsp; They say that therapists should
also be in therapy, do you think that actors

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.180
should be in therapy?&nbsp;

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:08.350
Because there are things that maybe you’re
blind to…they say we have this shadow self

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:09.400
that maybe we don’t see.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:15.450
Pascal:&nbsp; But I think acting or doing a film….for
him, I think he was going through something

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:20.400
when we did the film, a lot of things, but
he brought that, and it worked.&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.710
And sometimes it didn’t&nbsp; work and I had
to refocus him a little bit, but when sometimes

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:30.370
he was in this dark zone, he just fed from
it and it worked.&nbsp;

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:34.420
So I think screw therapy, just act in a film
or something like that.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.500
Tyler:&nbsp; I only laughed at the therapy line
because I think that maybe actors shouldn’t

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:44.780
be in therapy only because I feel like the
greatest gift that you can give audiences

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:51.280
in that vulnerable messy, chaotic, ugly side
of yourself.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.400
Pascal:&nbsp; And you want to discover yourself
takes after takes after takes and if you know

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.450
all the answers right away, there is nothing
left to be discovered.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:01.750
Tyler:&nbsp; I don’t even know if there is such
as thing as a well-balanced actor.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:03.760
Pascal:&nbsp; And I don’t trust it….

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.600
Tyler:&nbsp; Or artists, maybe in general.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.530
Film Courage:&nbsp; So if you’re one of these
keep calm and carry on type people, then that’s

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:10.940
not really going to translate?

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.220
Tyler:&nbsp; I mean if it works, then God bless
you.&nbsp;

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:18.061
I’m sure there are people who can prove
me wrong on that and if so, caps off to you.&nbsp;

00:04:18.061 --> 00:04:20.940
But I’d say for everybody else, let your
freak flag fly.&nbsp;

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:21.940
(Pascal and Tyler laughing..)

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:21.953
Question:&nbsp; Do you agree that an actor’s
personal problems manifest in their work?

